  urldate = {2025-12-12},
  abstract = {journal abstract This paper attempts to state the conditions under which consistency between attitude, cognitions, and behavior is to be expected. Attitudes are classified as positive or negative depending upon whether the individual feels favorable or unfavorable toward the attitude object or object of affective significance. Cognitions (beliefs about or perceptions of relationships) are classified as positive, negative, or null depending upon whether they are perceived as working toward the formation of a unit, working against the formation of a unit, or negating positive (or negative) relations. Behavior is classified as positive or negative depending upon how the individual evaluates it. 2 objects of affective significance associated with a cognition are referred to as a cognitive band. Such cognitive bands are consistent if the product of the signs is positive and inconsistent if the product of the signs is negative. Following the same multiplicative rule the triad of behavior, cognition, and object of affective significance is consistent if the product of the signs is positive and inconsistent if the product of the signs is negative. The movement toward triadic consistency and away from triadic inconsistency can occur in 2 basic situations, attitude-belief change following behavior change and behavior change following attitude-belief change. For numerous reasons the former sequence is much more likely to produce triadic consistency than is the latter. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2006 APA, all rights reserved)},
