  abstract = {A goal of educational efforts, whether in formal or informal learning environments, is to expand individuals' knowledge of some phenomenon. Promoting knowledge is commonly thought to be attained by scaffolding learners as active agents in integrating new information with their prior knowledge and experiences to construct meaning from the connections that emerge. This chapter reviews key theoretical models that represent different paradigmatic approaches to belief change and have inspired much contemporary research. It reviews recent empirical research from educational psychology and closely adjacent disciplines that are advancing the investigation of belief change, including cognitive, epistemic, emotional, and motivational factors and processes. Exemplar belief change interventions are described. Several issues in belief change research are explicated, including unintended consequences and other ethical considerations. The chapter concludes with research questions on belief change for the coming decade.},
